{
  "id": "urn:visionflow:page:ac7ee14748aca3780ae03c4508b643cd64cc5fe72b24f8ae909cd512949a183e",
  "title": "Attention",
  "slug": "attention",
  "public": true,
  "classIri": "urn:visionflow:owl:class:attention",
  "domain": "artificial-intelligence",
  "definition": "**Attention** is a differentiable content-based addressing mechanism for neural networks that computes a weighted combination of value vectors according to learned compatibility scores between a query vector and a set of key vectors, formalised in its most influential form as Scaled Dot-Product Attention (Vaswani et al. 2017) by the closed-form operator Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(QK^T / \u221ad_k) V where Q \u2208 \u211d^{n\u00d7d_k} is a matrix of n query vectors, K \u2208 \u211d^{m\u00d7d_k} is a matrix of m key vectors, V \u2208 \u211d^{m\u00d7d_v} is the matching matrix of value vectors, d_k is the key dimensionality and the \u221ad_k denominator prevents the softmax from saturating into one-hot regimes at high d_k where unscaled dot products acquire variance d_k and drive gradient collapse, originating in the additive (Bahdanau-style) formulation of Bahdanau, Cho & Bengio (ICLR 2015) \"Neural Machine Translation by Jointly Learning to Align and Translate\" which broke the fixed-vector bottleneck of Sutskever-Vinyals-Le (NeurIPS 2014) encoder-decoder seq2seq by allowing each decoder step to softly align over all encoder hidden states via score(s, h) = v^T tanh(W_s s + W_h h), refined into the simpler multiplicative (Luong) variant ",
